    "narrative": "Today\u2019s slate for Ghost Ride the WHIP centers on exploiting soft matchups to chase stolen base upside. The strongest plays on the board are **Mickey Moniak** (74.56) and **Corbin Carroll** (72.87). **Mickey Moniak** is our most efficient start, buoyed by a significant +14% platoon advantage against McGreevy, while **Corbin Carroll** remains a priority start despite a neutral matchup against Wrobleski. **Corbin Carroll**\u2019s elite sprint speed, combined with Wrobleski\u2019s slow delivery, makes him an essential anchor for our stolen base production today. While **Henry Bolte** (56.55) faces a tougher arm in Miller, his spot at the top of the order (+10%) and the wind-aided park boost ensure he stays in the lineup as a key source of runs and potential speed.\n\nThe most difficult bench decision involved **Cole Young** (42.59), who narrowly missed the cutoff. While his proximity to the 40.0 floor made him a candidate, his efficiency was suppressed by a difficult matchup against Seymour and a projected lineup spot that failed to offer the same leverage as our primary utility options. Elsewhere, we are monitoring the bottom of our lineup with caution: **Austin Riley** (44.08) and **Josh Naylor** (44.08) are trending toward the lower end of our starting thresholds. **Austin Riley** is struggling against the advanced metrics of Schlittler, while **Josh Naylor**\u2019s outlook is hampered by both a park penalty and a difficult left-on-left dynamic against Seymour.\n\nRisk management remains paramount as we navigate a high volume of \"Lineup Pending\" designations for **Gabriel Moreno** and **Isaac Paredes**. If official lineups force **Gabriel Moreno** or **Isaac Paredes** down the order, we will re-evaluate their efficiency scores to ensure they still clear our 40-point floor. With **Vinnie Pasquantino** and **James Wood** still sidelined on the IL, our depth is being tested; we are relying heavily on the versatility of players like **Zach McKinstry**, **Jose Caballero**, and **Edwin Arroyo** to rotate in as injury and rest management demand. We continue to prioritize stolen base potential in our bench spots, ensuring that **Trevor Story**, **Seiya Suzuki**, and **Dalton Rushing** remain ready to capitalize if a high-ceiling situational opportunity emerges.",
